    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Where Squarespace is the right call</h2>
    <p>Squarespace shines for design-led businesses such as photographers, boutiques, restaurants, and wedding venues. If your customers choose on looks more than on who ranks first in Google's map results for "[trade] near me," Squarespace was built for you. Use it.</p>

    <h2>Where Squarespace costs you leads</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Slower pages.</strong> Squarespace sites usually score 60&ndash;75 on Google's speed test for phones; a custom-coded site scores 95&ndash;100. Its editor code loads for every visitor and adds seconds on a phone, which is when visitors decide whether to stay.</li>
      <li><strong>Weaker local search.</strong> By default, Squarespace pages carry few of the business details Google reads (schema), and adding them by hand is hard. Its city pages are usually one template with the city name swapped, a pattern Google penalizes in its map results (the "local pack").</li>
      <li><strong>Forms tied to Squarespace.</strong> Submissions land in a Squarespace inbox, and sending them cleanly to your email or customer software takes a higher-tier plan. The form also doesn't sort requests by trade, urgency, or job type.</li>
      <li><strong>A familiar look.</strong> Designers spot a Squarespace site at once, and over time so do Google's checks for look-alike pages. Standing out in a crowded page of results decides who gets the click.</li>
    </ul>

